      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{(2-1)}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{1/2-1}-(-1){x}^{(-1-1)} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
